# USAGE: Make a hard link to this file at the root of your Vim/NeoVim
#        plugins checkout directory.
#
# The Vim/Neovim and OMR config assume that all plugins are stored
# under the same top-level directory. The projects checked out by
# the OMR config, and loaded by the Vim and Neovim plugin managers,
# are also listed below, so you can tell when your top-level plugin
# directory has unexpected changes.

# A top-level directory is used for storing plugins that are explicitly
# loaded using a package manager (e.g., by lazy.nvim or vim-plug).
# - This author used to use Vim's conventional ~/.vim/pack directory,
#   but then it's not as easy to disable plugins — you gotta `mv` a
#   plugin away from its ~/.vim/pack/*/start/ dir. to avoid Vim auto-
#   loading it.
#   - REFER: |packadd| |packloadall| |load-plugins| |rtp|
# - You'll see remnamts of start/ and opt/ directory usage (because
#   ~/.vim/pack/start and ~/.vim/pack/opt have special meaning to
#   packadd), but the author is phasing out those paths.
#   - Mostly so that local paths better match GH paths, e.g.,
#     for the repo https://github.com/org/plug, use the local
#     path org/plug and not org/start/plug or org/opt/plug.
#   - Because using a plugin manager, it doesn't matter where
#     you actually store each plugin.
#     - HSTRY: The start/ and opt/ usage is leftover from having
#       previously used ~/.vim/pack.
#     - MAYBE: Remove start/ and opt/ usage, e.g., combine
#         ~/.kit/nvim/<author>/start/*
#         ~/.kit/nvim/<author>/opt/*
#       into single
#         ~/.kit/nvim/<author>/*
#       - ORNOT: Moving dirs. seems like useless busy work.
#
# REFER: Note this project doesn't use a Vim plugin plugin to
# checkout or update the plugins, but relies upon a generic Git
# mutiple respository tool, `myrepos`:
#     https://myrepos.branchable.com/
# The author pairs this with a collection of libraries and actions:
#     https://github.com/landonb/ohmyrepos

# +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++ #

